  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nPersistent cold air advection across the region today has kept \ntemperatures slightly cooler than normal today, mainly in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s. Expect any lingering clouds to erode \nthis evening (outside of any lake effect clouds) as high \npressure expands across the area. This will allow temperatures \nto fall steadily after sunset, with overnight low temperatures \nin the upper teens to lower 20s in the outer suburbs and rural \nareas, with mid 20s to lower 30s in Chicago, warmest near the \nlakeshore. Tuesday will be similar to today with highs in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s. Winds will be lighter as the surface \nhigh remains in place across the region.\n\nLake effect snow showers have developed across portions of \nnorthern Lake Michigan today. The expectation is for this to \ncontinue south with time through this evening and overnight as \nnorthwest winds become more northerly. Have maintained slight \nchances for light rain or snow showers, mainly along the \nimmediate shore along and east of the IL-IN state line. Where \nlight rain does fall, expect temperatures to remain above \nfreezing.\n\nFocus continues to be on the expansive storm system anticipated\nto drive weather conditions across the eastern half of the \nCONUS over the Thanksgiving holiday and the days surrounding it.\nConfidence in the details remains quite low even just a few \ndays out owing to the complicated upper pattern and resultant \nphasing of various waves which will play a key role in if and \nwhere the western extent of precipitation will occur. In \naddition, the potential for heavy wet snow will largely be \ndriven by dynamic cooling effects as opposed to sub-freezing \ncold air advection, which adds further uncertainty. While \noperational model runs continue to bounce around in the overall \nsnowfall placement, ensembles have come into slightly better \nagreement the past few model cycles favoring higher snowfall \nprobabilities south and west of the Chicago metro area into \nportions of northeast Missouri and western and central Illinois.\n\nIt is still worth noting that some of the operational model \nruns and individual ensemble members do show slushy snow \naccumulations extending farther north into portions of our \nforecast area. Will continue to monitor these trends and adjust \nour forecasts and messaging accordingly if a larger consensus of\nmodels begin to converge toward a more northerly solution with\nlater updates.\n\nAs to be expected, forecast uncertainty exists in the wake of \nthe mid-week system, but ensembles favor a gradual moderating of\ntemperatures. In addition, guidance shows the potential for \nanother weather system somewhere in the region late this weekend\ninto early next week which could bring additional precipitation\nchances.\n\nPetr",
